{"custom_id": "prompt::17", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef smiles_to_compound(smiles, assign_descriptors=True):\n    it = iter(smiles)\n    mol = molecule()\n    try:\n        for token in it:\n            mol(token)\n        (result, _) = mol(None)\n    except KeyError as err:\n        raise ValueError('Unsupported Symbol: {}'.format(err))\n    result.graph.remove_node(0)\n    logger.debug(result)\n    if assign_descriptors:\n        molutil.assign_descriptors(result)\n    return result\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nConvert SMILES text to compound object\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: SMILES with unsupported format\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `smiles_to_compound` takes SMILES string and optional boolean flag. It creates molecule object from SMILES tokens. If token unsupported, raises ValueError. Removes node 0 from graph. Logs debug info. Optionally assigns descriptors. Returns processed molecule.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::18", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef delete(self, message_id, reservation_id=None, subscriber_name=None):\n    url = 'queues/%s/messages/%s' % (self.name, message_id)\n    qitems = {}\n    if reservation_id is not None:\n        qitems['reservation_id'] = reservation_id\n    if subscriber_name is not None:\n        qitems['subscriber_name'] = subscriber_name\n    body = json.dumps(qitems)\n    result = self.client.delete(url=url, body=body, headers={'Content-Type': 'application/json'})\n    return result['body']\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nExecute an HTTP request to delete a message from queue.\n\n        Arguments:\n        message_id -- The ID of the message to be deleted.\n        reservation_id -- Reservation Id of the message. Reserved message could not be deleted without reservation Id.\n        subscriber_name -- This is required to acknowledge push after long-processing of message is finished.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `delete` deletes a message from a queue. It takes three parameters: `message_id`, `reservation_id`, and `subscriber_name`. If `reservation_id` or `subscriber_name` is provided, they are included in the request body. The function sends a DELETE request to the specified URL with the JSON-encoded body containing any provided parameters. The response body is returned.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or