{"custom_id": "prompt::55", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef run(self, host, port=25, with_ssl=False):\n    try:\n        dns_rec = self._lookup(host, port)\n        self._connect(dns_rec)\n        if with_ssl:\n            self._wrap_ssl()\n        banner = self._get_banner()\n        self._check_banner(banner)\n    except Exception:\n        (exc_type, exc_value, exc_tb) = sys.exc_info()\n        self.results['Exception-Type'] = str(exc_type.__name__)\n        self.results['Exception-Value'] = str(exc_value)\n        self.results['Exception-Traceback'] = repr(traceback.format_exc())\n    finally:\n        self._close(with_ssl)\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nExecutes a single health check against a remote host and port. This\n        method may only be called once per object.\n\n        :param host: The hostname or IP address of the SMTP server to check.\n        :type host: str\n        :param port: The port number of the SMTP server to check.\n        :type port: int\n        :param with_ssl: If ``True``, SSL will be initiated before attempting\n                         to get the banner message.\n        :type with_ssl: bool\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `run` starts. Takes `host`, `port`, and `with_ssl`. Tries to lookup DNS record. Connects using that record. If `with_ssl`, wraps connection in SSL. Gets banner from server. Checks banner validity. Catches any exceptions. Records exception type, value, traceback. Finally closes connection, respecting SSL status. Ends function.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::56", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef add_pass_thru(pass_thrus):\n\n    def meth(self, node):\n        pass\n\n    def dec(cls):\n        for element_name in pass_thrus:\n            for meth_prefix in ('visit_', 'depart_'):\n                meth_name = meth_prefix + element_name\n                if hasattr(cls, meth_name):\n                    raise ValueError('method name {} already defined'.format(meth_name))\n                setattr(cls, meth_name, meth)\n        return cls\n    return dec\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nDecorator adds explicit pass-through visit and depart methods\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `add_pass_thru` takes list of strings `pass_thrus`. It defines inner function `meth` that does nothing. Another inner function `dec` iterates over each string in `pass_thrus`, and for each string, it checks all possible method names (`visit_elementName` and `depart_elementName`). If any of these methods already exist in class `cls`, it raises an error. Otherwise, it adds `meth` to `cls` under the appropriate method name. Finally, `dec` returns modified class `cls`. The outer function returns `dec`.\n\nNext step: Use this decorator to automatically add placeholder methods to classes based on provided element names.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise