{"custom_id": "prompt::75", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef tokenize(self, text, stream=False, wakati=False, baseform_unk=True, dotfile=''):\n    if self.wakati:\n        wakati = True\n    if stream:\n        return self.__tokenize_stream(text, wakati, baseform_unk, '')\n    elif dotfile and len(text) < Tokenizer.MAX_CHUNK_SIZE:\n        return list(self.__tokenize_stream(text, wakati, baseform_unk, dotfile))\n    else:\n        return list(self.__tokenize_stream(text, wakati, baseform_unk, ''))\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nu\"\"\"\n        Tokenize the input text.\n\n        :param text: unicode string to be tokenized\n        :param stream: (Optional) if given True use stream mode. default is False.\n        :param wakati: (Optinal) if given True returns surface forms only. default is False.\n        :param baseform_unk: (Optional) if given True sets base_form attribute for unknown tokens. default is True.\n        :param dotfile: (Optional) if specified, graphviz dot file is output to the path for later visualizing of the lattice graph. This option is ignored when the input length is larger than MAX_CHUNK_SIZE or running on stream mode.\n\n        :return: list of tokens (stream=False, wakati=False) or token generator (stream=True, wakati=False) or list of string (stream=False, wakati=True) or string generator (stream=True, wakati=True)\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nTokenize `text`. If `stream`, process text in chunks. If `wakati`, use Wakati mode. If `dotfile` and text length under limit, use `dotfile`. Otherwise, use default settings. Return tokenized list.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::76", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef remove_permissions(self, value, role='any'):\n    permission_list = self.client.list_permissions(self.id)\n    key = 'emailAddress' if '@' in value else 'domain'\n    filtered_id_list = [p['id'] for p in permission_list if p[key] == value and (p['role'] == role or role == 'any')]\n    for permission_id in filtered_id_list:\n        self.client.remove_permission(self.id, permission_id)\n    return filtered_id_list\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nRemove permissions from a user or domain.\n\n        :param value: User or domain to remove permissions from\n        :type value: str\n        :param role: (optional) Permission to remove. Defaults to all\n                     permissions.\n        :type role: str\n\n        Example::\n\n            # Remove Otto's write permission for this spreadsheet\n            sh.remove_permissions('otto@example.com', role='writer')\n\n            # Remove all Otto's permissions for this spreadsheet\n            sh.remove_permissions('otto@example.com')\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nList permissions first. Check if email or domain. Filter by value and role. Remove matching permissions. Return removed IDs.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::77", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef _get_horoscope(self, day='today'):\n    if not is_valid_day(day):\n        raise HoroscopeException('Invalid day. Allowed days: [today|yesterday|tomorrow]')\n    horoscope = ''.join([str(s).strip() for s in self.tree.xpath('//*[@id=\"%s\"]/p/text()' % day)])\n    if day is 'yesterday':\n        date = self.date_today - timedelta(days=1)\n    elif day is 'today':\n        date = self.date_today\n    elif day is 'tomorrow':\n        date = self.date_today + timedelta(days=1)\n    return {'date': date.strftime('%Y-%m-%d'), 'sunsign': self.sunsign.capitalize(), 'horoscope': horoscope + '(c) Kelli Fox, The Astrologer, http://new.theastrologer.com', 'meta': self._get_horoscope_meta(day), 'credit': '(c) Kelli Fox, The Astrologer, http://new.theastrologer.com'}\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\ngets a horoscope from site html\n\n        :param day: day for which to get horoscope. Default is 'today'\n\n        :returns: dictionary of horoscope details\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `_get_horoscope` fetches horoscope data for specified day. Checks if day valid using `is_valid_day`. Retrieves horoscope text via XPath from XML tree. Adjusts date based on input ('today', 'yesterday', 'tomorrow'). Returns dictionary with date, sunsign, horoscope content, metadata, and credit info.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::78", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef parse_url(url, warning=True):\n    parsed = urllib_parse.urlparse(url)\n    query = urllib_parse.parse_qs(parsed.query)\n    is_gdrive = parsed.hostname == 'drive.google.com'\n    is_download_link = parsed.path.endswith('/uc')\n    file_id = None\n    if is_gdrive and 'id' in query:\n        file_ids = query['id']\n        if len(file_ids) == 1:\n            file_id = file_ids[0]\n    match = re.match('^/file/d/(.*?)/view$', parsed.path)\n    if match:\n        file_id = match.groups()[0]\n    if is_gdrive and (not is_download_link):\n        warnings.warn('You specified Google Drive Link but it is not the correct link to download the file. Maybe you should try: {url}'.format(url='https://drive.google.com/uc?id={}'.format(file_id)))\n    return (file_id, is_download_link)\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nParse URLs especially for Google Drive links.\n\n    file_id: ID of file on Google Drive.\n    is_download_link: Flag if it is download link of Google Drive.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nParse URL [extract components]. Check if Google Drive link [verify hostname and path]. Extract file ID [from query parameter or path pattern]. Warn if incorrect download link [if Google Drive but not download path]. Return file ID and download status [tuple of results].\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::79", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef sort_snps(snps):\n    sorted_list = sorted(snps['chrom'].unique(), key=_natural_sort_key)\n    if 'PAR' in sorted_list:\n        sorted_list.remove('PAR')\n        sorted_list.append('PAR')\n    if 'MT' in sorted_list:\n        sorted_list.remove('MT')\n        sorted_list.append('MT')\n    snps['chrom'] = snps['chrom'].astype(CategoricalDtype(categories=sorted_list, ordered=True))\n    snps = snps.sort_values(['chrom', 'pos'])\n    snps['chrom'] = snps['chrom'].astype(object)\n    return snps\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nSort SNPs based on ordered chromosome list and position.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nSorts SNPs by chromosome and position. Removes PAR and MT chromosomes, then reinserts them at end. Converts chromosome column to categorical type with sorted order. Sorts dataframe by chromosome and position. Resets chromosome column to object type. Returns sorted dataframe.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::80", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef verification_digit(numbers):\n    a = sum(numbers[::2])\n    b = a * 3\n    c = sum(numbers[1::2])\n    d = b + c\n    e = d % 10\n    if e == 0:\n        return e\n    return 10 - e\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nReturns the verification digit for a given numbre.\n\n        The verification digit is calculated as follows:\n\n        * A = sum of all even-positioned numbers\n        * B = A * 3\n        * C = sum of all odd-positioned numbers\n        * D = B + C\n        * The results is the smallset number N, such that (D + N) % 10 == 0\n\n        NOTE: Afip's documentation seems to have odd an even mixed up in the\n        explanation, but all examples follow the above algorithm.\n\n        :param list(int) numbers): The numbers for which the digits is to be\n            calculated.\n        :return: int\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nSum even-indexed numbers. Multiply by three. Sum odd-indexed numbers. Add both sums. Modulo ten result. If zero, return zero. Else, subtract from ten. Return final digit.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::81", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef as_dict(self):\n    out = {}\n    for prop in self:\n        propval = getattr(self, prop)\n        if hasattr(propval, 'for_json'):\n            out[prop] = propval.for_json()\n        elif isinstance(propval, list):\n            out[prop] = [getattr(x, 'for_json', lambda : x)() for x in propval]\n        elif isinstance(propval, (ProtocolBase, LiteralValue)):\n            out[prop] = propval.as_dict()\n        elif propval is not None:\n            out[prop] = propval\n    return out\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nReturn a dictionary containing the current values\n        of the object.\n\n        Returns:\n            (dict): The object represented as a dictionary\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `as_dict` converts object properties into dictionary format. Iterates over object's properties, checks their types, and applies specific conversion logic:\n\n- If property has method `for_json`, calls it.\n- If property is list, iterates over items, applying similar logic.\n- If property is instance of `ProtocolBase` or `LiteralValue`, calls `as_dict` method.\n- Otherwise, uses property value directly.\n\nReturns resulting dictionary.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
