{"custom_id": "prompt::104", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef get_cas(key_options, global_options, item):\n    if item:\n        ign_cas = get_option('ignore_cas', key_options, globals(), False)\n        return 0 if ign_cas else item.cas\n    else:\n        try:\n            cas = key_options['cas']\n        except KeyError:\n            try:\n                cas = global_options['cas']\n            except KeyError:\n                return 0\n        if not cas:\n            return 0\n        return cas\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nGet the CAS from various inputs. This will properly honor\n    the ``ignore_cas`` flag, if present.\n\n    :param key_options: Key specific options\n    :param global_options: Global options\n    :param item: The item\n    :return: The cas, or 0 if no cas\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `get_cas` checks if `item` exists. If it does, it gets option 'ignore_cas' from `key_options`. If 'ignore_cas' is true, it returns 0; otherwise, it returns `item.cas`. If `item` doesn't exist, it tries to get 'cas' from `key_options`, then from `global_options`. If 'cas' isn't found in either, it returns 0. If 'cas' is found but is false, it also returns 0. Otherwise, it returns the value of 'cas'.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::105", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef reset(self, fid=0):\n    self._checkid(fid)\n    self._fitids[fid]['solved'] = False\n    self._fitids[fid]['haserr'] = False\n    if not self._fitids[fid]['looped']:\n        return self._fitproxy.reset(fid)\n    else:\n        self._fitids[fid]['looped'] = False\n    return True\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nReset the object's resources to its initialized state.\n\n        :param fid: the id of a sub-fitter\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `reset` resets fit ID. Checks ID validity. Sets solved and haserr flags to false. If looped flag is true, sets it to false. Returns result of `_fitproxy.reset(fid)` if not looped. Otherwise, returns true.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::106", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef parse(cls, requester, entry):\n    if not type(entry) is dict:\n        return entry\n    for (key_to_parse, cls_to_parse) in six.iteritems(cls.parser):\n        if key_to_parse in entry:\n            entry[key_to_parse] = cls_to_parse.parse(requester, entry[key_to_parse])\n    return cls(requester, **entry)\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nTurns a JSON object into a model instance.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `parse` takes three arguments: `cls`, `requester`, and `entry`. If `entry` is not a dictionary, it returns `entry` as is. Otherwise, it iterates over each key-value pair in `cls.parser`. For each key found in `entry`, it calls the `parse` method of the corresponding class from `cls.parser` with `requester` and the value associated with that key in `entry`. After processing all keys, it creates an instance of `cls` using `requester` and the updated `entry` dictionary.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::107", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef _encode_filename_header(self, filename):\n    user_agent = self.request.META.get('HTTP_USER_AGENT', None)\n    if 'WebKit' in user_agent:\n        return u'filename={}'.format(filename).encode('utf-8')\n    elif 'MSIE' in user_agent:\n        url_encoded = quote(filename.encode('utf-8')).replace('attachment', 'a%74tachment')\n        return 'filename={}'.format(url_encoded).encode('utf-8')\n    else:\n        rfc2231_filename = quote(filename.encode('utf-8'))\n        return \"filename*=UTF-8''{}\".format(rfc2231_filename).encode('utf-8')\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nThe filename, encoded to use in a ``Content-Disposition`` header.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `_encode_filename_header` encodes a file name based on the user agent. If the user agent contains 'WebKit', it encodes the filename directly. For 'MSIE', it URL encodes and modifies the string. Otherwise, it uses RFC 2231 encoding. Returns encoded filename.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
