{"custom_id": "prompt::135", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef get_sentence(start=None, depth=7):\n    if not GRAMMAR:\n        return 'Please set a GRAMMAR file'\n    start = start if start else GRAMMAR.start()\n    if isinstance(start, Nonterminal):\n        productions = GRAMMAR.productions(start)\n        if not depth:\n            terminals = [p for p in productions if not isinstance(start, Nonterminal)]\n            if len(terminals):\n                production = terminals\n        production = random.choice(productions)\n        sentence = []\n        for piece in production.rhs():\n            sentence += get_sentence(start=piece, depth=depth - 1)\n        return sentence\n    else:\n        return [start]\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nfollow the grammatical patterns to generate a random sentence\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `get_sentence` generates a sentence using grammar rules. It starts with an optional non-terminal symbol and a recursion depth limit. If no grammar is set, it returns a message asking to set one. If the start symbol is not provided, it uses the start symbol from the grammar. If the start symbol is a non-terminal, it selects a production rule randomly and recursively generates sentences for each part of the rule until reaching terminal symbols. If the start symbol is already a terminal, it directly returns that symbol. The generated sentence is returned as a list of tokens.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::136", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef add_codes(err_cls):\n\n    class ErrorsWithCodes(object):\n\n        def __getattribute__(self, code):\n            msg = getattr(err_cls, code)\n            return '[{code}] {msg}'.format(code=code, msg=msg)\n    return ErrorsWithCodes()\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nAdd error codes to string messages via class attribute names.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `add_codes` takes a class `err_cls`. It creates and returns a new class `ErrorsWithCodes`.\n\nIn `ErrorsWithCodes`, method `__getattribute__` is defined. This method gets called when an attribute is accessed on instances of `ErrorsWithCodes`.\n\nWhen an attribute is accessed, it uses `getattr` to get the value from `err_cls` using the attribute name as the key. Then, it formats this value into a string that includes both the attribute name (`code`) and its corresponding value (`msg`). The formatted string is then returned.\n\nThis allows instances of `ErrorsWithCodes` to behave like dictionaries where keys are attribute names and values are formatted strings combining the attribute name and its corresponding value from `\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::137", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\nasync def start_component_in_thread(executor, workload: CoroutineFunction[T], *args: Any, loop=None, **kwargs: Any) -> Component[T]:\n    loop = loop or asyncio.get_event_loop()\n    (commands_a, commands_b) = pipe(loop=loop)\n    (events_a, events_b) = pipe(loop=loop)\n    commands_b = ConcurrentPipeEnd(commands_b, loop=loop)\n    events_b = ConcurrentPipeEnd(events_b, loop=loop)\n    _workload = workload(*args, commands=commands_b, events=events_b, **kwargs)\n    future = cast(_Future[T], loop.run_in_executor(executor, asyncio.run, _workload))\n    component = Component[T](commands_a, events_a, future)\n    await component.wait_for_start()\n    return component\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\n\\\n    Starts the passed `workload` with additional `commands` and `events` pipes.\n    The workload will be executed on an event loop in a new thread; the thread is provided by `executor`.\n\n    This function is not compatible with `ProcessPoolExecutor`,\n    as references between the workload and component are necessary.\n\n    Be careful when using an executor with a maximum number of threads,\n    as long running workloads may starve other tasks.\n    Consider using a dedicated executor that can spawn at least as many threads\n    as concurrent long-running tasks are expected.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nStarts a component in a thread. Uses an executor and coroutine function. Sets up pipes for commands and events. Runs workload in executor. Waits for component to start. Returns component.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::138", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef reduce_to_unit(divider):\n    for unit_size in range(1, len(divider) // 2 + 1):\n        length = len(divider)\n        unit = divider[:unit_size]\n        divider_item = divider[:unit_size * (length // unit_size)]\n        if unit * (length // unit_size) == divider_item:\n            return unit\n    return divider\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nReduce a repeating divider to the smallest repeating unit possible.\n\n    Note: this function is used by make-div\n    :param divider: the divider\n    :return: smallest repeating unit possible\n    :rtype: str\n\n    :Example:\n    'XxXxXxX' -> 'Xx'\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `reduce_to_unit(divider)` takes string `divider`. It tries to find smallest repeating substring that can make up whole string. If found, returns that substring; otherwise, returns original string.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "prompt::139", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef _inner_product(y, yr, psd):\n    fmin = 40.0\n    fmax = 2000.0\n    fs = 16384.0\n    y = 1.0 / fs * y\n    yr = 1.0 / fs * yr\n    p = np.multiply(y, np.conjugate(yr)) / psd\n    product = max(abs(fs * sp.ifft(np.multiply(y, np.conjugate(yr)) / psd)))\n    return product\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nCompute inner product between two time domain waveforms, weighted by noisecurve.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `_inner_product` calculates an inner product using input arrays `y`, `yr`, and scalar `psd`. It scales inputs by `fs`, computes the product of `y` and conjugate of `yr` divided by `psd`, then finds the maximum absolute value of the inverse Fourier transform of this product scaled by `fs`. Returns this maximum value.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
