{"custom_id": "prompt::154", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef GTax(x, ax):\n    slc0 = (slice(None),) * ax\n    xg = np.roll(x, 1, axis=ax) - x\n    xg[slc0 + (slice(0, 1),)] = -x[slc0 + (slice(0, 1),)]\n    xg[slc0 + (slice(-1, None),)] = x[slc0 + (slice(-2, -1),)]\n    return xg\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nCompute transpose of gradient of `x` along axis `ax`.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    x : array_like\n      Input array\n    ax : int\n      Axis on which gradient transpose is to be computed\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    xg : ndarray\n      Output array\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `GTax` takes two parameters: `x`, an array, and `ax`, an integer representing an axis. It calculates the gradient of `x` along the specified axis using finite differences. The function first creates a slice object `slc0` that selects all elements along all axes except the one specified by `ax`. Then, it computes the gradient by shifting `x` one position along `ax` and subtracting the original `x`. At the boundaries, it adjusts the values to ensure correct gradient calculation at the edges. Finally, it returns the computed gradient array `xg`.\n\nNext step: Understand how the gradient is calculated and why adjustments are made at the boundaries.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
