{"custom_id": "conceptor_alone::73", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef callgraph(G, stmt_list):\n    func_list = []\n    for stmt in stmt_list:\n        try:\n            G.add_node(stmt.head.ident.name)\n            func_list.append(stmt)\n        except:\n            pass\n    for func in func_list:\n        assert isinstance(func, node.function)\n        func_name = func.head.ident.name\n        for s in node.postorder(func):\n            if s.__class__ is node.funcall and s.func_expr.__class__ is node.ident:\n                G.add_edge(func_name, s.func_expr.name)\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nBuild callgraph of func_list, ignoring\n    built-in functions\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `callgraph` function constructs a call graph from a list of statements using a given graph object `G`. It iterates over each statement in `stmt_list`, adding nodes to the graph for functions that haven't been added yet and collecting these function statements into `func_list`. Then, it processes each function in `func_list`, ensuring they are instances of `node.function`. For each function, it retrieves its name and traverses the function's body in post-order (depth-first search). If a statement is a function call (`node.funcall`) and the called function is identified by an identifier (`node.ident`), it adds an edge from the current function's name to the called function's name in the graph `G`.\n\n**Created\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\"