{"custom_id": "proper_alone::92", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef valuedispatch(func):\n    registry = {}\n\n    def dispatch(value):\n        return registry.get(value, func)\n\n    def register(value, func=None):\n        if func is None:\n            return lambda f: register(value, f)\n        registry[value] = func\n        return func\n\n    def wrapper(*args, **kw):\n        return dispatch(args[0])(*args, **kw)\n    wrapper.register = register\n    wrapper.dispatch = dispatch\n    wrapper.registry = MappingProxyType(registry)\n    update_wrapper(wrapper, func)\n    return wrapper\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nDecorates a function to dispatch handler of the value of the first\n    argument.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `valuedispatch` function creates a decorator that allows you to handle different values differently based on their type or content. It uses a registry dictionary to map specific values to corresponding functions. The `dispatch` function looks up the appropriate function in the registry and calls it with the provided arguments. If no matching value is found, it defaults to calling the original function passed to `valuedispatch`.\n\nThe `register` function adds new mappings to the registry. It can be used as both a regular function and a decorator for convenience. The `wrapper` function acts as the actual decorator, applying the dispatch logic when called. It also includes references to the `register`, `dispatch`, and `registry` functions so they can be accessed later\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correc