{"custom_id": "proper_alone::154", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef GTax(x, ax):\n    slc0 = (slice(None),) * ax\n    xg = np.roll(x, 1, axis=ax) - x\n    xg[slc0 + (slice(0, 1),)] = -x[slc0 + (slice(0, 1),)]\n    xg[slc0 + (slice(-1, None),)] = x[slc0 + (slice(-2, -1),)]\n    return xg\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nCompute transpose of gradient of `x` along axis `ax`.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    x : array_like\n      Input array\n    ax : int\n      Axis on which gradient transpose is to be computed\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    xg : ndarray\n      Output array\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe function `GTax` takes two arguments: `x`, which is an array, and `ax`, which specifies the axis along which to operate. It calculates the gradient tax of `x` with respect to the specified axis. Here's how it works:\n\n1. Creates a tuple `slc0` of slices for all axes except the one specified by `ax`.\n2. Computes the gradient of `x` along axis `ax` by shifting elements one position forward and subtracting the original values.\n3. Sets the first element of the gradient along axis `ax` to be negative of the first element of `x`.\n4. Sets the last element of the gradient along axis `ax` to be equal to the second-to-last\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON obje