{"custom_id": "stolfo_alone::154", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef GTax(x, ax):\n    slc0 = (slice(None),) * ax\n    xg = np.roll(x, 1, axis=ax) - x\n    xg[slc0 + (slice(0, 1),)] = -x[slc0 + (slice(0, 1),)]\n    xg[slc0 + (slice(-1, None),)] = x[slc0 + (slice(-2, -1),)]\n    return xg\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nCompute transpose of gradient of `x` along axis `ax`.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    x : array_like\n      Input array\n    ax : int\n      Axis on which gradient transpose is to be computed\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    xg : ndarray\n      Output array\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `GTax` function calculates the gradient of an array along a specified axis. It first shifts the array values by one position to the right and subtracts the original array from it. Then, it sets the first value to negative and the last value to the second-to-last value. This effectively computes the gradient at each point.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
