{"custom_id": "stolfo_alone::162", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef initial_step(self, phase, x, y):\n    self.x[0] = x\n    self.y[0] = y\n    self.phase[0] = phase\n    if self.MODE == CordicMode.ROTATION:\n        if phase > 0.5:\n            self.x[0] = -x\n            self.phase[0] = phase - 1.0\n        elif phase < -0.5:\n            self.x[0] = -x\n            self.phase[0] = phase + 1.0\n    elif self.MODE == CordicMode.VECTORING:\n        if x < 0.0 and y > 0.0:\n            self.x[0] = -x\n            self.y[0] = -y\n            self.phase[0] = 1.0\n        elif x < 0.0 and y < 0.0:\n            self.x[0] = -x\n            self.y[0] = -y\n            self.phase[0] = -1.0\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nTransform input to the CORDIC working quadrants\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThis function initializes the starting values for `x`, `y`, and `phase`. It then checks the current mode (`self.MODE`). If in rotation mode, it adjusts `x` and `phase` based on whether `phase` is greater than or less than 0.5. In vectoring mode, it adjusts `x` and `y` based on their signs to ensure they are positive.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "stolfo_alone::163", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef dispatch_queue(loader):\n    queue = loader._queue\n    loader._queue = []\n    max_batch_size = loader.max_batch_size\n    if max_batch_size and max_batch_size < len(queue):\n        chunks = get_chunks(queue, max_batch_size)\n        for chunk in chunks:\n            dispatch_queue_batch(loader, chunk)\n    else:\n        dispatch_queue_batch(loader, queue)\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nGiven the current state of a Loader instance, perform a batch load\n    from its current queue.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThis function takes a `loader` as an argument. It empties the `_queue` attribute of the `loader`, storing it in a variable called `queue`. Then, it checks if there is a `max_batch_size` set on the `loader`. If there is and it's less than the length of `queue`, it divides `queue` into smaller batches using `get_chunks()`. For each batch, it calls `dispatch_queue_batch()` with the `loader` and the batch. If no `max_batch_size` or it's not applicable, it directly calls `dispatch_queue_batch()` with the `loader` and the entire `queue`.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
