cursive_dependencies`. Then, it filters out ignored packages and creates a dictionary to store dependency information. It also gathers the currently installed packages and their versions. For each dependency, it attempts to find the version from the installed packages; if not found, it warns that the package is not installed and sets the version to `None`. Next, it fetches additional data about each package using `research_package` and updates the dependency dictionary with this data. Finally, it returns an ordered dictionary of dependencies\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
