ent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "mg_gradient_trained_probe_nll_alone::3", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef tables(self):\n    _tables = set()\n    for attr in six.itervalues(self.__dict__):\n        if isinstance(attr, list):\n            for item in attr:\n                if isinstance(item, Node):\n                    _tables |= item.tables()\n        elif isinstance(attr, Node):\n            _tables |= attr.tables()\n    return _tables\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nGeneric method that does a depth-first search on the node attributes.\n\n        Child classes should override this method for better performance.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `tables` method iterates over all attributes of an object using `six.itervalues(self.__dict__)`. If an attribute is a list, it checks each item; if any item is an instance of `Node`, it recursively calls the `tables()` method on that item and updates `_tables` with the results using the union operator (`|=`). If an attribute is directly an instance of `Node`, it calls its `tables()` method and updates `_tables`. Finally, it returns `_tables`, which contains all tables found within nested nodes. This method is used to gather all tables from complex structures where nodes may contain other nodes or lists of nodes.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with O