{"custom_id": "mg_gradient_trained_probe_nll_alone::135", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef get_sentence(start=None, depth=7):\n    if not GRAMMAR:\n        return 'Please set a GRAMMAR file'\n    start = start if start else GRAMMAR.start()\n    if isinstance(start, Nonterminal):\n        productions = GRAMMAR.productions(start)\n        if not depth:\n            terminals = [p for p in productions if not isinstance(start, Nonterminal)]\n            if len(terminals):\n                production = terminals\n        production = random.choice(productions)\n        sentence = []\n        for piece in production.rhs():\n            sentence += get_sentence(start=piece, depth=depth - 1)\n        return sentence\n    else:\n        return [start]\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nfollow the grammatical patterns to generate a random sentence\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `get_sentence` function generates sentences using a grammar. If no grammar is set, it prompts you to do so. It starts from a given symbol or uses the grammar's default start symbol. If the current symbol is non-terminal, it picks a random production rule and recursively generates parts of the sentence until reaching terminal symbols. When at a terminal, it adds that symbol to the growing sentence. This process repeats until all parts are generated and combined into a complete sentence. The recursion depth limits how many levels deep it can go before stopping and returning partial results. Finally, it returns the generated sentence as a list of tokens.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  cor