{"custom_id": "mg_gradient_trained_probe_nll_alone::162", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef initial_step(self, phase, x, y):\n    self.x[0] = x\n    self.y[0] = y\n    self.phase[0] = phase\n    if self.MODE == CordicMode.ROTATION:\n        if phase > 0.5:\n            self.x[0] = -x\n            self.phase[0] = phase - 1.0\n        elif phase < -0.5:\n            self.x[0] = -x\n            self.phase[0] = phase + 1.0\n    elif self.MODE == CordicMode.VECTORING:\n        if x < 0.0 and y > 0.0:\n            self.x[0] = -x\n            self.y[0] = -y\n            self.phase[0] = 1.0\n        elif x < 0.0 and y < 0.0:\n            self.x[0] = -x\n            self.y[0] = -y\n            self.phase[0] = -1.0\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nTransform input to the CORDIC working quadrants\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `initial_step` function sets up initial values for coordinates (`x`, `y`) and a phase angle in a Cordic algorithm. If operating in rotation mode, it adjusts the sign of `x` based on the phase value and normalizes the phase within [-0.5, 0.5]. In vectoring mode, it reflects both `x` and `y` and sets the phase to 1.0 or -1.0 depending on the quadrant of the input coordinates. This prepares the starting point for iterative calculations in the Cordic method. [End] [Next step: Continue with next part of code.] [End of explanation.]\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English g