{"custom_id": "mg_gradient_trained_probe_nll_prompt::55", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef run(self, host, port=25, with_ssl=False):\n    try:\n        dns_rec = self._lookup(host, port)\n        self._connect(dns_rec)\n        if with_ssl:\n            self._wrap_ssl()\n        banner = self._get_banner()\n        self._check_banner(banner)\n    except Exception:\n        (exc_type, exc_value, exc_tb) = sys.exc_info()\n        self.results['Exception-Type'] = str(exc_type.__name__)\n        self.results['Exception-Value'] = str(exc_value)\n        self.results['Exception-Traceback'] = repr(traceback.format_exc())\n    finally:\n        self._close(with_ssl)\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nExecutes a single health check against a remote host and port. This\n        method may only be called once per object.\n\n        :param host: The hostname or IP address of the SMTP server to check.\n        :type host: str\n        :param port: The port number of the SMTP server to check.\n        :type port: int\n        :param with_ssl: If ``True``, SSL will be initiated before attempting\n                         to get the banner message.\n        :type with_ssl: bool\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `run` starts server connection. It looks up DNS record first. Then connects using that info. If SSL needed, wraps connection next. Gets banner and checks it. Any errors caught, notes type, value, and traceback. Finally closes connection always. Continue? [yes/no]\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
{"custom_id": "mg_gradient_trained_probe_nll_prompt::56", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef add_pass_thru(pass_thrus):\n\n    def meth(self, node):\n        pass\n\n    def dec(cls):\n        for element_name in pass_thrus:\n            for meth_prefix in ('visit_', 'depart_'):\n                meth_name = meth_prefix + element_name\n                if hasattr(cls, meth_name):\n                    raise ValueError('method name {} already defined'.format(meth_name))\n                setattr(cls, meth_name, meth)\n        return cls\n    return dec\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nDecorator adds explicit pass-through visit and depart methods\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `add_pass_thru` takes list of elements. It defines method that does nothing. Then it creates decorator that adds this method to class for each element, prefixed with 'visit_' or 'depart_'. If method already exists, it raises error. Finally, it returns modified class. Next step is using this decorator on classes needing these methods. [End]\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
