{"custom_id": "mg_gradient_trained_probe_nll_prompt::135", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef get_sentence(start=None, depth=7):\n    if not GRAMMAR:\n        return 'Please set a GRAMMAR file'\n    start = start if start else GRAMMAR.start()\n    if isinstance(start, Nonterminal):\n        productions = GRAMMAR.productions(start)\n        if not depth:\n            terminals = [p for p in productions if not isinstance(start, Nonterminal)]\n            if len(terminals):\n                production = terminals\n        production = random.choice(productions)\n        sentence = []\n        for piece in production.rhs():\n            sentence += get_sentence(start=piece, depth=depth - 1)\n        return sentence\n    else:\n        return [start]\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nfollow the grammatical patterns to generate a random sentence\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nFunction `get_sentence` starts with optional parameters `start` and `depth`. If grammar isn't set, it asks to configure one. It picks a starting point and reduces depth each time recursing. If starting point is non-terminal, it chooses a random production rule and recursively generates sentences from right-hand side elements until reaching terminal symbols. If starting point is terminal, it returns that symbol directly. This process builds up a sentence gradually. Next step depends on whether current element is non-terminal or terminal. Continue generating until desired depth reached or all possible paths explored. Return generated sentence. End. [End]\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so garbled it fails to\n  read as a genuine explanation; true otherwise.\n\nRespond with ONLY a JSON object, no other text: {\"correct\": <0|1|2>, \"coherent\": <true|false>}"}]}}
