{"custom_id": "mg_dim_probe_mse_alone::67", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef get_photometry(self, brightest=False, min_unc=0.02, convert=True):\n    if brightest:\n        row = self.brightest\n    else:\n        row = self.closest\n    if not hasattr(self, 'conversions'):\n        convert = False\n    if convert:\n        bands = self.conversions\n    else:\n        bands = self.bands.keys()\n    d = {}\n    for b in bands:\n        if convert:\n            key = b\n            (mag, dmag) = getattr(self, b)(brightest=brightest)\n        else:\n            key = self.bands[b]\n            (mag, dmag) = (row[b], row['e_{}'.format(b)])\n        d[key] = (mag, max(dmag, min_unc))\n    return d\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nReturns dictionary of photometry of closest match\n\n        unless brightest is True, in which case the brightest match.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `get_photometry` method retrieves photometric data from an object. It can fetch either the brightest or closest photometry based on the `brightest` parameter. If no conversions are available and `convert` is set to `True`, it defaults to using raw band names. For each band, it calculates the magnitude and uncertainty, ensuring the uncertainty is at least `min_unc`. The results are stored in a dictionary with band names as keys and tuples of magnitude and uncertainty as values.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non