{"custom_id": "mg_conc_probe_mse_alone::107", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef _encode_filename_header(self, filename):\n    user_agent = self.request.META.get('HTTP_USER_AGENT', None)\n    if 'WebKit' in user_agent:\n        return u'filename={}'.format(filename).encode('utf-8')\n    elif 'MSIE' in user_agent:\n        url_encoded = quote(filename.encode('utf-8')).replace('attachment', 'a%74tachment')\n        return 'filename={}'.format(url_encoded).encode('utf-8')\n    else:\n        rfc2231_filename = quote(filename.encode('utf-8'))\n        return \"filename*=UTF-8''{}\".format(rfc2231_filename).encode('utf-8')\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nThe filename, encoded to use in a ``Content-Disposition`` header.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `_encode_filename_header` method encodes a file name for use in HTTP headers based on the user agent of the request. It checks if the user agent contains 'WebKit' (common in Chrome and Safari), 'MSIE' (Internet Explorer), or neither to determine the appropriate encoding method:\n\n1. If the user agent is WebKit, it directly formats the filename with UTF-8 encoding.\n2. If the user agent is MSIE, it URL-encodes the filename, replaces 'attachment' with 'a%74tachment', and then formats it.\n3. For other user agents, it uses RFC 2231 encoding, which supports international characters more robustly.\n\nThis ensures compatibility across different browsers when downloading\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"c