{"custom_id": "mg_conc_probe_nll_alone::73", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef callgraph(G, stmt_list):\n    func_list = []\n    for stmt in stmt_list:\n        try:\n            G.add_node(stmt.head.ident.name)\n            func_list.append(stmt)\n        except:\n            pass\n    for func in func_list:\n        assert isinstance(func, node.function)\n        func_name = func.head.ident.name\n        for s in node.postorder(func):\n            if s.__class__ is node.funcall and s.func_expr.__class__ is node.ident:\n                G.add_edge(func_name, s.func_expr.name)\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nBuild callgraph of func_list, ignoring\n    built-in functions\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `callgraph` function takes a graph `G` and a list of statements `stmt_list`. It iterates over each statement, adding nodes to the graph for functions it encounters and collecting these function statements in `func_list`.\n\nFor each function in `func_list`, it asserts that the item is a function node. Then, it traverses the function's body using a postorder traversal. If it finds a function call (`node.funcall`) where the called function is an identifier (`node.ident`), it adds an edge from the current function name to the called function name in the graph `G`. This constructs a call graph showing which functions call others.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate, repetitive, non-English gibberish, or so 