{"custom_id": "mg_conc_probe_nll_alone::135", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef get_sentence(start=None, depth=7):\n    if not GRAMMAR:\n        return 'Please set a GRAMMAR file'\n    start = start if start else GRAMMAR.start()\n    if isinstance(start, Nonterminal):\n        productions = GRAMMAR.productions(start)\n        if not depth:\n            terminals = [p for p in productions if not isinstance(start, Nonterminal)]\n            if len(terminals):\n                production = terminals\n        production = random.choice(productions)\n        sentence = []\n        for piece in production.rhs():\n            sentence += get_sentence(start=piece, depth=depth - 1)\n        return sentence\n    else:\n        return [start]\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nfollow the grammatical patterns to generate a random sentence\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `get_sentence` function generates a sentence using grammar rules. It takes two optional parameters: `start`, which specifies where to begin generating the sentence, and `depth`, which limits how deep the recursion can go. If no grammar is set, it returns an error message. The function checks if the starting point is a non-terminal symbol; if so, it selects a production rule randomly and recursively generates sentences from the right-hand side of that rule until reaching terminal symbols or hitting the depth limit. If the starting point is already a terminal symbol, it simply returns that symbol as part of the sentence.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function