{"custom_id": "mg_conc_probe_nll_alone::172", "method": "POST", "url": "/v1/chat/completions", "body": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 50, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are grading an automatically generated explanation of a Python function.\n\nFunction:\n```python\ndef _descriptor_names(self):\n    descriptor_names = []\n    for name in dir(self):\n        try:\n            attr = getattr(type(self), name)\n            if isinstance(attr, DJANGO_RELATED_FIELD_DESCRIPTOR_CLASSES):\n                descriptor_names.append(name)\n        except AttributeError:\n            pass\n    return descriptor_names\n```\n\nReference explanation (written by the original developer, for grading only):\nAttributes which are Django descriptors. These represent a field\n        which is a one-to-many or many-to-many relationship that is\n        potentially defined in another model, and doesn't otherwise appear\n        as a field on this model.\n\nCandidate explanation to grade:\nThe `_descriptor_names` method finds names of attributes that are related fields in a Django model. It iterates over all attributes of the current object using `dir(self)`. For each attribute, it tries to get its type from the class using `getattr(type(self), name)`. If the attribute is an instance of `DJANGO_RELATED_FIELD_DESCRIPTOR_CLASSES`, which presumably contains classes for Django's related field descriptors like ForeignKey or ManyToManyField, it adds the attribute name to the list `descriptor_names`. If any attribute doesn't exist, it catches the `AttributeError` and continues with the next one. Finally, it returns the list of descriptor names.\n\nScore the candidate explanation on two axes:\n- \"correct\": 0 if it is wrong or misleading about what the function does, 1 if it is vague or only partially\n  correct, 2 if it correctly captures the function's actual behavior (wording may differ from the reference).\n- \"coherent\": false if the text is degenerate